   </title>
   <document-link format="APPLICATION/PDF" url="https://www.adb.org/sites/default/files/project-documents/57018/57018-001-rrp-en.pdf">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Reports and Recommendations of the President</narrative>
    </title>
    <category code="A08"></category>
    <language code="EN"></language>
    <document-date iso-date="2025-09-30+08:00"></document-date>
   </document-link>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1 The government approved with immediate effect, a law and regulation defining the responsibility of local government for collection of retribution fees and stipulating that fees collected should be used for the related service, to increase revenues and budget allocation for improved waste collection services.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.2. Local governments commenced implementation of regulation mandating the use of digital payment systems to improve the efficiency of collection of retribution fees,d and MOHA directed local governments on appropriate budget allocation in line with the targets set out in the National Policy and Strategy on Management of Household Waste and Household-like Waste.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.3. The MOEF approved, with immediate effect, regulation incentivizing good performance of waste banks and improved cooperation between waste banks, recyclers, and producers in line with 3R (reduce, reuse, and recycle) principles, and at least one local government issued guidance on handling of residual waste including diapers and sanitary napkins to reduce the impact of harmful items of marine debris.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.4. The government approved the 2022 eNDC with an increased role of the waste sector in achieving Indonesia&#x2019;s climate change mitigation targets by raising the GHG emission reduction targets for the waste sector.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.5. The MMAF approved with immediate effect regulation on pollution prevention which, (i) obliges any actors whose activities might result in pollution or damage to fisheries and the marine environment to prepare and implement prevention and rehabilitation plans, (ii) prevents fishing vessels from disposing of fishing gear in the sea and mandating the provision of waste collection facilities on board, to increase protection of the marine environment.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.6. The MMAF implemented its commitment under the NAP by certifying at least 18% of targeted fishing ports as meeting international standards on waste management including on solid waste management and wastewater management.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.7. The MMAF established and operationalized an assistance scheme for improved waste facilities and waste infrastructure for effective waste handling for communities in small islands and coastal areas vulnerable to the impacts of climate change, by providing guidance for communities on accessing government funds and offering capacity building assistance.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">24 May 2024 | News Release: https://www.adb.org/news/adb-strengthens-plastic-marine-debris-reduction-program-indonesia-500-million-loan</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
